const AY_SYSTEMS=[{key:"ayurveda",name:"Ayurveda",icon:"🌿",meaning:'ayus (life) + veda (knowledge) — "the knowledge of life"',origin:"Indian subcontinent",period:"Textual tradition consolidated roughly 1st millennium BCE to 1st millennium CE",basis:"Tridosha — vata, pitta and kapha — with sapta dhatu (seven tissues) and agni (digestive fire)",diagnosis:"Ashtavidha pariksha (eightfold examination), including nadi pariksha (pulse), jihva (tongue) and mutra (urine)",materia:"Predominantly plant-based, with mineral and metallic preparations (rasa shastra) and animal products",degree:"BAMS — Bachelor of Ayurvedic Medicine and Surgery",tagline:"The largest of the six systems by practitioner numbers and institutional footprint",desc:"Ayurveda organises health around balance between three functional principles rather than around named diseases. Its classical literature is unusually detailed on surgery, on the classification of drugs and on regimen — the Sushruta Samhita describes reconstructive procedures including rhinoplasty, and the Charaka Samhita devotes as much attention to diet, sleep and daily routine as to medicine.",branches:["Kayachikitsa (internal medicine)","Shalya tantra (surgery)","Shalakya tantra (eye, ear, nose, throat)","Kaumarabhritya (paediatrics)","Agada tantra (toxicology)","Bhuta vidya (psychiatry)","Rasayana (rejuvenation)","Vajikarana (reproductive health)"],note:"Some traditional rasa shastra preparations contain mercury, lead or arsenic. These are the subject of ongoing safety concern, and preparations from unverified sources have been linked to heavy-metal poisoning."},{key:"yoga",name:"Yoga & Naturopathy",icon:"🧘",meaning:'yuj — "to yoke" or "to join"; naturopathy from natura + pathos',origin:"Indian subcontinent; naturopathy adapted from a 19th-century European movement",period:"Yoga: philosophical formulation by roughly the early centuries CE. Naturopathy: adopted in India from the 1890s",basis:"Yoga: the eight limbs of Patanjali. Naturopathy: the body's self-healing capacity, with disease framed as accumulated toxicity",diagnosis:"Constitutional and lifestyle assessment rather than a distinct diagnostic system",materia:"No pharmacopoeia — the interventions are posture, breath, diet, fasting, hydrotherapy and mud therapy",degree:"BNYS — Bachelor of Naturopathy and Yogic Sciences",tagline:"The only pair in AYUSH with no pharmacology at all",desc:"Yoga and naturopathy are grouped administratively but come from different places. Yoga is an Indian philosophical and practical discipline whose therapeutic application is comparatively recent; Patanjali's Yoga Sutras treat asana as one limb of eight, and the postural practice most people now mean by \"yoga\" is largely a twentieth-century development. Naturopathy arrived from Europe and was taken up in India partly through Gandhi's advocacy.",branches:["Yama & Niyama (ethical observances)","Asana (posture)","Pranayama (breath regulation)","Pratyahara (withdrawal of the senses)","Dharana (concentration)","Dhyana (meditation)","Samadhi (absorption)","Naturopathic modalities: diet, fasting, hydrotherapy, mud, sun"],note:"Yoga has the strongest modern evidence base of the six for specific outcomes such as flexibility, balance and some measures of stress. That evidence does not extend to every claim made for it."},{key:"unani",name:"Unani",icon:"⚗️",meaning:'From "Ionian" — the Arabic name for Greek',origin:"Greco-Arabic; reached India with the Delhi Sultanate",period:"Greek roots from around the 5th century BCE; formalised in the Islamic world from the 9th century CE; in India from the 13th century",basis:"Four humours — dam (blood), balgham (phlegm), safra (yellow bile) and sauda (black bile) — and the four qualities",diagnosis:"Nabz (pulse), baul (urine), baraz (stool), and observation of temperament (mizaj)",materia:"Plant, mineral and animal drugs, with compound formulations and regimenal therapy (ilaj bil tadbeer)",degree:"BUMS — Bachelor of Unani Medicine and Surgery",tagline:"Greek humoral medicine, preserved and extended in India",desc:"Unani descends from the humoral medicine of Hippocrates and Galen, transmitted through Arabic scholarship — above all Ibn Sina's Canon of Medicine — and brought to India by the Delhi Sultanate. It is the one system in AYUSH whose theoretical parent tradition died out in the country of its origin: European medicine abandoned humoral theory, while in India the system continued to develop, and it retains a substantial institutional presence, particularly in Uttar Pradesh, Delhi and Hyderabad.",branches:["Ilaj bil dawa (pharmacotherapy)","Ilaj bil ghiza (dietotherapy)","Ilaj bil tadbeer (regimenal therapy)","Ilaj bil yad (surgery)","Hijama (cupping)","Fasd (venesection)","Dalak (massage)","Hammam (therapeutic bathing)"],note:"The Hakim family lineages of Delhi — particularly the Sharif Manzil and Ajmal Khan traditions — were central to Unani's institutional survival under colonial rule."},{key:"siddha",name:"Siddha",icon:"🔥",meaning:'From siddhi — "attainment" or "perfection"',origin:"Tamil-speaking South India",period:"Traditionally attributed to the eighteen Siddhars; textual tradition largely in Tamil",basis:"The same three humours as Ayurveda under Tamil names — vatham, pitham, kabam — with a distinctive five-element framework",diagnosis:"Envagai thervu — eightfold examination including naadi (pulse), sparisam (touch), naa (tongue), niram (colour), mozhi (voice), vizhi (eye), malam (stool) and moothiram (urine)",materia:"Notably heavy in mineral and metallic preparations — parpam, chendooram, chunnam — alongside plant drugs",degree:"BSMS — Bachelor of Siddha Medicine and Surgery",tagline:"A Tamil system with the most mineral-heavy pharmacy of the six",desc:"Siddha is often described as a southern variant of Ayurveda, which understates the difference. Its literature is in Tamil rather than Sanskrit, it is attributed to a distinct lineage of eighteen Siddhars rather than to the Vedic tradition, and its pharmacy leans much further toward processed minerals and metals. Its practice remains concentrated in Tamil Nadu, Puducherry and parts of Kerala and Sri Lanka.",branches:["Kayakarpam (rejuvenation and longevity)","Varmam (pressure-point therapy)","Thokkanam (manipulative therapy)","Muppu (a distinctive Siddha preparation)","Parpam & chendooram (calcined mineral preparations)","Attaikkadi (leech therapy)"],note:"The mineral and metallic preparations central to Siddha require specific processing (suddhi) and are the part of the system where quality control matters most."},{key:"homoeopathy",name:"Homoeopathy",icon:"💧",meaning:'homoios (like) + pathos (suffering) — "like cures like"',origin:"Germany",period:"Founded by Samuel Hahnemann around 1796; reached India in the 1830s",basis:"The law of similars, the single remedy, and the minimum dose administered in serial dilution",diagnosis:"Extended case-taking covering physical, emotional and constitutional characteristics, matched against a repertory",materia:"Serially diluted and succussed preparations from plant, mineral and animal sources",degree:"BHMS — Bachelor of Homoeopathic Medicine and Surgery",tagline:"German in origin, and Indian by adoption more thoroughly than anywhere else",desc:"Homoeopathy is the outlier in AYUSH: it is neither Indian in origin nor ancient. It was developed in Germany at the end of the eighteenth century, arrived in India in the 1830s, and was taken up so widely — particularly in Bengal — that India now has more homoeopathic practitioners and institutions than any other country. Its inclusion in AYUSH reflects that scale of adoption rather than any claim of Indian origin.",branches:["Classical (single remedy, minimum dose)","Constitutional prescribing","Repertorisation","Materia medica study","Organon-based case management"],note:"Homoeopathy's central premise — that therapeutic effect persists through dilutions beyond which no molecule of the original substance remains — is not accepted by mainstream pharmacology, and systematic reviews have not found effects distinguishable from placebo. This is stated here because a page describing the system should describe the state of the evidence too."},{key:"sowarigpa",name:"Sowa-Rigpa",icon:"🏔️",meaning:'Tibetan for "the science of healing"',origin:"Tibetan plateau and the Indian Himalaya",period:"Textual tradition from around the 8th–12th centuries CE; recognised by Indian statute in 2010",basis:"Three nyes-pa — rLung, mKhris-pa and Bad-kan — broadly parallel to the tridosha, within a Buddhist philosophical frame",diagnosis:"Pulse reading, urine analysis and questioning, with unusually elaborate pulse and urine protocols",materia:"High-altitude Himalayan plants, minerals and precious-stone preparations",degree:"BSRMS — Bachelor of Sowa-Rigpa Medicine and Surgery",tagline:"The sixth system, and the reason the acronym under-counts",desc:"Sowa-Rigpa is practised across Ladakh, Himachal Pradesh, Sikkim, Arunachal Pradesh and the Tibetan exile community, where its practitioners are called amchi. It was given statutory recognition in India in 2010 through an amendment to the Indian Medicine Central Council Act — which is why the AYUSH acronym, fixed earlier, does not have a letter for it. Its principal text, the Gyud-Zhi or Four Tantras, is the reference work of the tradition.",branches:["Diet (zas)","Behaviour (spyod-lam)","Medicine (sman)","External therapy (dpyad)","Golden needle therapy","Moxibustion","Precious pill (rinchen rilbu) preparations"],note:"Sowa-Rigpa's materia medica draws heavily on high-altitude Himalayan plants, several of which are now threatened by over-collection."}],AY_COMPARISON=[{system:"Ayurveda",origin:"Indian subcontinent",period:"1st millennium BCE onward",theory:"Tridosha: vata, pitta, kapha",diagnosis:"Ashtavidha pariksha",materia:"Plant-dominant, with rasa shastra minerals",degree:"BAMS"},{system:"Yoga & Naturopathy",origin:"India / adapted European",period:"Early centuries CE / 1890s in India",theory:"Eight limbs; self-healing capacity",diagnosis:"Constitutional and lifestyle assessment",materia:"None — no pharmacopoeia",degree:"BNYS"},{system:"Unani",origin:"Greco-Arabic, via the Delhi Sultanate",period:"Greek roots 5th c. BCE; India 13th c. CE",theory:"Four humours and four qualities",diagnosis:"Nabz, baul, baraz",materia:"Plant, mineral and animal compounds",degree:"BUMS"},{system:"Siddha",origin:"Tamil South India",period:"Tamil textual tradition",theory:"Vatham, pitham, kabam",diagnosis:"Envagai thervu (eightfold)",materia:"Strongly mineral and metallic",degree:"BSMS"},{system:"Homoeopathy",origin:"Germany",period:"Founded c. 1796; India from the 1830s",theory:"Law of similars; minimum dose",diagnosis:"Extended case-taking and repertorisation",materia:"Serial dilutions of plant, mineral, animal sources",degree:"BHMS"},{system:"Sowa-Rigpa",origin:"Tibetan plateau and Indian Himalaya",period:"c. 8th–12th c. CE; Indian statute 2010",theory:"Three nyes-pa: rLung, mKhris-pa, Bad-kan",diagnosis:"Pulse and urine analysis",materia:"Himalayan plants, minerals, precious stones",degree:"BSRMS"}],AY_CATEGORIES=[{key:"purification",name:"Purification & Detox",icon:"🌊"},{key:"external",name:"External Therapy",icon:"💆"},{key:"formulation",name:"Formulation Type",icon:"🫙"},{key:"diagnostic",name:"Diagnostic Method",icon:"🔍"},{key:"regimen",name:"Regimen & Lifestyle",icon:"🕰️"},{key:"manual",name:"Manual & Movement",icon:"🤲"}],AY_PRACTICES=[{name:"Vamana",system:"ayurveda",category:"purification",term:"Therapeutic emesis",desc:"The first of the five panchakarma procedures — induced vomiting, classically indicated for kapha-predominant conditions. Preceded by oleation and sudation over several days."},{name:"Virechana",system:"ayurveda",category:"purification",term:"Therapeutic purgation",desc:"Induced purgation, classically indicated where pitta is judged to predominate. Like vamana, it is preceded by preparatory procedures rather than administered on its own."},{name:"Basti",system:"ayurveda",category:"purification",term:"Medicated enema",desc:"Described in the classical texts as the single most important panchakarma procedure. Two main forms: niruha (decoction-based) and anuvasana (oil-based)."},{name:"Nasya",system:"ayurveda",category:"purification",term:"Nasal administration",desc:"Medicated oils or powders administered through the nostrils, classically directed at conditions of the head, neck and sinuses."},{name:"Raktamokshana",system:"ayurveda",category:"purification",term:"Bloodletting",desc:"The fifth panchakarma procedure, performed by venesection or with leeches. Its indications are narrow and it is the least commonly performed of the five."},{name:"Abhyanga",system:"ayurveda",category:"external",term:"Oil massage",desc:"Whole-body massage with warm medicated oil, used both as a preparatory procedure before panchakarma and as a daily regimen practice in its own right."},{name:"Shirodhara",system:"ayurveda",category:"external",term:"Oil stream on the forehead",desc:"A continuous stream of warm oil poured onto the forehead for a set duration. The best known Ayurvedic procedure outside India, and often the only one people have heard of."},{name:"Swedana",system:"ayurveda",category:"external",term:"Sudation therapy",desc:"Induced sweating through steam, hot fomentation or herbal poultice, used to mobilise what the preparatory oleation has loosened."},{name:"Pizhichil",system:"ayurveda",category:"external",term:"Oil bath",desc:"A Kerala procedure in which warm medicated oil is squeezed continuously over the body from cloth, combined with simultaneous massage."},{name:"Kizhi",system:"ayurveda",category:"external",term:"Poultice fomentation",desc:"Heated bundles of herbs, rice, sand or powder applied to the body. Widely used in Kerala practice for musculoskeletal complaints."},{name:"Rasayana",system:"ayurveda",category:"regimen",term:"Rejuvenation therapy",desc:"One of the eight classical branches. A regimen of formulations and conduct intended to support vitality and slow decline, rather than to treat a named illness."},{name:"Dinacharya",system:"ayurveda",category:"regimen",term:"Daily regimen",desc:"The classical prescription for the structure of a day — waking time, oral and nasal care, oil application, exercise, bathing and meals — treated as preventive medicine."},{name:"Ritucharya",system:"ayurveda",category:"regimen",term:"Seasonal regimen",desc:"Adjustment of diet and conduct across six seasons, on the principle that what suits the body in one season may not in another."},{name:"Nadi Pariksha",system:"ayurveda",category:"diagnostic",term:"Pulse examination",desc:"Assessment of the radial pulse at three finger positions, read for qualities held to indicate the state of the three doshas. Practitioner-dependent and not standardised across schools."},{name:"Ashtavidha Pariksha",system:"ayurveda",category:"diagnostic",term:"Eightfold examination",desc:"Pulse, urine, stool, tongue, voice, touch, eye and general appearance — the standard Ayurvedic examination protocol."},{name:"Prakriti Assessment",system:"ayurveda",category:"diagnostic",term:"Constitutional typing",desc:"Classification of an individual's constitution by dosha predominance, which the texts treat as fixed from birth and as the basis for individualised regimen."},{name:"Churna",system:"ayurveda",category:"formulation",term:"Powder",desc:"Dried and finely powdered single or compound drugs. Triphala, a three-fruit compound, is the most widely used example."},{name:"Kwatha",system:"ayurveda",category:"formulation",term:"Decoction",desc:"Coarse drug boiled in water and reduced to a set fraction. One of the primary dosage forms of classical Ayurveda."},{name:"Ghrita",system:"ayurveda",category:"formulation",term:"Medicated ghee",desc:"Drugs processed into clarified butter, used where a lipid-soluble vehicle is intended. Brahmi ghrita is among the best known."},{name:"Asava & Arishta",system:"ayurveda",category:"formulation",term:"Fermented preparations",desc:"Self-generated alcoholic preparations produced by fermenting drugs with a sugar source. The alcohol is a product of the process rather than an added ingredient."},{name:"Bhasma",system:"ayurveda",category:"formulation",term:"Calcined preparation",desc:"Metals and minerals repeatedly purified and incinerated. The category where quality control matters most, since inadequate processing leaves toxic residues."},{name:"Asana",system:"yoga",category:"manual",term:"Posture",desc:"The third of Patanjali's eight limbs. In the Sutras it refers to a steady seated posture; the extensive postural repertoire practised today is largely a twentieth-century development."},{name:"Pranayama",system:"yoga",category:"manual",term:"Breath regulation",desc:"The fourth limb — regulation of inhalation, retention and exhalation. Several techniques have specific cautions and are not appropriate for everyone."},{name:"Dhyana",system:"yoga",category:"regimen",term:"Meditation",desc:"The seventh limb, sustained attention on a single object. The limb with the largest volume of modern clinical research behind it."},{name:"Shatkarma",system:"yoga",category:"purification",term:"Six cleansing acts",desc:"Neti, dhauti, nauli, basti, kapalabhati and trataka — hatha yoga purification practices, several of which carry real risks if performed without instruction."},{name:"Yoga Nidra",system:"yoga",category:"regimen",term:"Guided relaxation",desc:"A systematic guided relaxation performed lying down, in a state between waking and sleep."},{name:"Hydrotherapy",system:"yoga",category:"external",term:"Water treatment",desc:"Naturopathic application of water at varying temperatures — baths, packs, compresses, sitz baths — as the principal external modality of the system."},{name:"Mud Therapy",system:"yoga",category:"external",term:"Mud application",desc:"Application of prepared mud as pack or full-body coating, a standard naturopathic modality in Indian practice."},{name:"Upavasa",system:"yoga",category:"regimen",term:"Therapeutic fasting",desc:"Structured fasting under supervision, central to naturopathic practice. Contraindicated in a range of conditions and not a self-administered intervention."},{name:"Nabz",system:"unani",category:"diagnostic",term:"Pulse diagnosis",desc:"Unani pulse examination, assessed across ten qualities including rate, rhythm, force and volume. The most elaborate pulse protocol of the six systems."},{name:"Hijama",system:"unani",category:"external",term:"Cupping",desc:"Application of suction cups, dry or wet. A regimenal therapy (ilaj bil tadbeer) rather than a pharmacological one."},{name:"Fasd",system:"unani",category:"external",term:"Venesection",desc:"Therapeutic bloodletting from a vein, historically central to humoral practice and now used narrowly."},{name:"Dalak",system:"unani",category:"manual",term:"Massage",desc:"Therapeutic massage with defined techniques and oils, classified under regimenal therapy."},{name:"Hammam",system:"unani",category:"external",term:"Therapeutic bath",desc:"Structured bathing in graded temperatures, a formal treatment modality in Unani rather than a hygiene practice."},{name:"Majun",system:"unani",category:"formulation",term:"Confection",desc:"A semi-solid compound formulation with drugs incorporated into a sugar or honey base."},{name:"Sharbat",system:"unani",category:"formulation",term:"Syrup",desc:"A concentrated sweet liquid preparation, one of the standard Unani dosage forms."},{name:"Kushta",system:"unani",category:"formulation",term:"Calcined mineral",desc:"The Unani equivalent of bhasma — minerals and metals reduced by repeated incineration, with the same quality-control considerations."},{name:"Envagai Thervu",system:"siddha",category:"diagnostic",term:"Eightfold examination",desc:"The Siddha diagnostic protocol: naadi, sparisam, naa, niram, mozhi, vizhi, malam and moothiram. Its urine examination (neerkuri and neikuri) is distinctive."},{name:"Neikuri",system:"siddha",category:"diagnostic",term:"Oil-on-urine reading",desc:"A drop of sesame oil is placed on a urine sample and the way it spreads is read diagnostically. Specific to Siddha and not found in the other systems."},{name:"Varmam",system:"siddha",category:"manual",term:"Pressure-point therapy",desc:"Manipulation of defined points on the body, related to the Tamil martial tradition and taught within closed lineages."},{name:"Thokkanam",system:"siddha",category:"manual",term:"Manipulative therapy",desc:"A set of Siddha massage and manipulation techniques applied for musculoskeletal and neurological complaints."},{name:"Parpam",system:"siddha",category:"formulation",term:"Calcined powder",desc:"A white calcined mineral or metallic preparation, produced by repeated purification and incineration cycles."},{name:"Chendooram",system:"siddha",category:"formulation",term:"Red calcined preparation",desc:"A red-coloured calcined preparation, distinguished from parpam by colour and by the process used."},{name:"Kayakarpam",system:"siddha",category:"regimen",term:"Longevity regimen",desc:"The Siddha rejuvenation tradition, combining specific preparations with dietary and behavioural discipline."},{name:"Case Taking",system:"homoeopathy",category:"diagnostic",term:"Extended interview",desc:"A long structured interview covering physical symptoms, mental state, preferences and history, aimed at a total symptom picture rather than a disease label."},{name:"Repertorisation",system:"homoeopathy",category:"diagnostic",term:"Symptom-to-remedy matching",desc:"Systematic matching of the recorded symptom picture against a repertory index to shortlist a remedy."},{name:"Potentisation",system:"homoeopathy",category:"formulation",term:"Serial dilution and succussion",desc:"Repeated dilution with vigorous shaking at each step. At common potencies the dilution exceeds the point at which any molecule of the original substance would remain."},{name:"Mother Tincture",system:"homoeopathy",category:"formulation",term:"Base preparation",desc:"The undiluted alcoholic extract from which potencies are prepared, denoted Q or θ."},{name:"Pulse & Urine Reading",system:"sowarigpa",category:"diagnostic",term:"Tsa and chuwa examination",desc:"The core Sowa-Rigpa diagnostic pair, with detailed protocols governing timing, the practitioner's own state, and what the patient may consume beforehand."},{name:"Golden Needle Therapy",system:"sowarigpa",category:"external",term:"Ser-khab",desc:"Application of a heated gold needle at defined points, a distinctive external therapy of the Tibetan system."},{name:"Moxibustion",system:"sowarigpa",category:"external",term:"Me-btsa",desc:"Application of heat by burning prepared material at specific points on the body."},{name:"Rinchen Rilbu",system:"sowarigpa",category:"formulation",term:"Precious pills",desc:"Multi-ingredient preparations incorporating processed precious stones and metals, taken under specific conditions and among the most elaborate formulations in any of the six systems."}],AY_TEXTS=[{title:"Charaka Samhita",system:"Ayurveda",author:"Attributed to Charaka, redacting Agnivesha",lang:"Sanskrit",desc:"The foundational text of internal medicine. As much on diet, conduct, ethics and the training of physicians as on drugs."},{title:"Sushruta Samhita",system:"Ayurveda",author:"Attributed to Sushruta",lang:"Sanskrit",desc:"The surgical compendium. Describes instruments, procedures and reconstructive surgery including the forehead-flap rhinoplasty later adapted in Europe."},{title:"Ashtanga Hridaya",system:"Ayurveda",author:"Vagbhata",lang:"Sanskrit",desc:"A concise synthesis of the two earlier compendia, and for many practitioners the most-used classical reference of the three."},{title:"Madhava Nidana",system:"Ayurveda",author:"Madhavakara",lang:"Sanskrit",desc:"The standard classical text on diagnosis and the classification of disease."},{title:"Bhava Prakasha",system:"Ayurveda",author:"Bhavamishra",lang:"Sanskrit",desc:"A later compendium whose nighantu section is a principal reference for materia medica."},{title:"Yoga Sutras",system:"Yoga",author:"Patanjali",lang:"Sanskrit",desc:"The formulation of the eight limbs. Notably brief on posture — a reminder that classical yoga was not primarily a postural practice."},{title:"Hatha Yoga Pradipika",system:"Yoga",author:"Svatmarama",lang:"Sanskrit",desc:"The principal hatha text, covering asana, pranayama, mudra and the shatkarma cleansing practices."},{title:"Canon of Medicine (Al-Qanun fi al-Tibb)",system:"Unani",author:"Ibn Sina (Avicenna)",lang:"Arabic",desc:"The reference work of Unani, and a standard European medical text for several centuries after its translation."},{title:"Kitab al-Hawi",system:"Unani",author:"Al-Razi (Rhazes)",lang:"Arabic",desc:"A vast clinical compendium notable for recording case observations alongside received theory."},{title:"Agastyar Texts",system:"Siddha",author:"Attributed to Agastyar",lang:"Tamil",desc:"A body of works attributed to the foremost of the eighteen Siddhars, covering medicine, alchemy and longevity."},{title:"Theraiyar Texts",system:"Siddha",author:"Attributed to Theraiyar",lang:"Tamil",desc:"Siddha works particularly associated with diagnosis and with the treatment of specific disease groups."},{title:"Organon of the Medical Art",system:"Homoeopathy",author:"Samuel Hahnemann",lang:"German",desc:"The founding statement of homoeopathic principle, revised by its author across six editions."},{title:"Materia Medica Pura",system:"Homoeopathy",author:"Samuel Hahnemann",lang:"German",desc:"The record of the original provings — symptom pictures produced by administering substances to healthy volunteers."},{title:"Gyud-Zhi (Four Tantras)",system:"Sowa-Rigpa",author:"Traditional; attributed to Yuthok Yonten Gonpo",lang:"Tibetan",desc:"The root text of Sowa-Rigpa, organised as four tantras covering theory, diagnosis, pharmacology and practice."}],AY_INSTITUTIONS=[{icon:"🏛️",name:"Ministry of AYUSH",year:"2014",desc:"Created in November 2014 by upgrading the Department of AYUSH, which had itself been formed in 2003 from the Department of Indian Systems of Medicine and Homoeopathy (1995)."},{icon:"⚖️",name:"National Commission for Indian System of Medicine",year:"2020",desc:"The statutory regulator for Ayurveda, Unani, Siddha and Sowa-Rigpa education and practice, replacing the Central Council of Indian Medicine."},{icon:"⚖️",name:"National Commission for Homoeopathy",year:"2020",desc:"The corresponding statutory regulator for homoeopathic education and practice."},{icon:"🔬",name:"CCRAS",year:"1978",desc:"Central Council for Research in Ayurvedic Sciences — the apex research body for Ayurveda, running a network of institutes across the country."},{icon:"🔬",name:"CCRUM",year:"1978",desc:"Central Council for Research in Unani Medicine, coordinating clinical, drug-standardisation and literary research."},{icon:"🔬",name:"CCRS",year:"2010",desc:"Central Council for Research in Siddha, separated from the combined Ayurveda–Siddha council to give Siddha a dedicated body."},{icon:"🔬",name:"CCRH",year:"1978",desc:"Central Council for Research in Homoeopathy, responsible for drug proving, clinical research and documentation."},{icon:"📕",name:"Pharmacopoeia Commission for Indian Medicine & Homoeopathy",year:"2010",desc:"Publishes the official pharmacopoeias and formularies that set identity, purity and strength standards for AYUSH drugs."},{icon:"🎓",name:"All India Institute of Ayurveda",year:"2017",desc:"An apex referral, teaching and research institute in New Delhi, intended as the Ayurvedic counterpart to AIIMS."},{icon:"🌏",name:"WHO Global Traditional Medicine Centre",year:"2022",desc:"Established at Jamnagar, Gujarat — the WHO's only global centre dedicated to traditional medicine."},{icon:"📖",name:"Traditional Knowledge Digital Library",year:"2001",desc:"A searchable database of documented Indian traditional medicine, built to give patent examiners prior art and block misappropriation. It was central to overturning patents on turmeric wound-healing and neem."},{icon:"💊",name:"AYUSH Drug Regulation",year:"Ongoing",desc:"AYUSH drugs are licensed under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 with a separate schedule and their own manufacturing standards (GMP under Schedule T)."}],AY_TIMELINE=[{year:"Antiquity",title:"The classical compendia",desc:"The Charaka and Sushruta Samhitas take the form in which they were transmitted, establishing internal medicine and surgery as distinct branches."},{year:"13th c.",title:"Unani arrives",desc:"Greco-Arabic medicine reaches India with the Delhi Sultanate and begins to develop an independent Indian tradition."},{year:"1830s",title:"Homoeopathy reaches India",desc:"Introduced in the 1830s and taken up rapidly, particularly in Bengal, where it acquires an institutional base within decades."},{year:"1835",title:"Colonial suppression",desc:"Official teaching of Indian medical systems in government institutions is discontinued in favour of Western medicine, pushing the systems into private and lineage-based transmission."},{year:"1916",title:"Ayurvedic and Unani Tibbia College",desc:"Hakim Ajmal Khan establishes the college in Delhi, one of the most consequential institutional revivals of the colonial period."},{year:"1946",title:"Chopra Committee",desc:"The first systematic post-war official review of indigenous systems of medicine, recommending integration and state support."},{year:"1970",title:"Indian Medicine Central Council Act",desc:"Establishes statutory regulation of education and practice in Ayurveda, Unani and Siddha."},{year:"1973",title:"Homoeopathy Central Council Act",desc:"Brings homoeopathic education and practice under equivalent statutory regulation."},{year:"1995",title:"A dedicated department",desc:"The Department of Indian Systems of Medicine and Homoeopathy is created within the Ministry of Health."},{year:"2001",title:"TKDL launched",desc:"The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library begins documenting Indian traditional medicine in a form patent offices can search."},{year:"2003",title:"Renamed AYUSH",desc:"The department is renamed AYUSH, fixing the acronym before Sowa-Rigpa is recognised."},{year:"2010",title:"Sowa-Rigpa recognised",desc:"The Himalayan system is given statutory recognition through an amendment to the Indian Medicine Central Council Act."},{year:"2014",title:"Ministry of AYUSH",desc:"AYUSH is upgraded from a department to a full ministry in November 2014."},{year:"2016",title:"Yoga inscribed by UNESCO",desc:"Yoga is added to the UNESCO Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity."},{year:"2020",title:"New regulators",desc:"The NCISM and NCH replace the earlier central councils, restructuring regulation of education and practice."},{year:"2022",title:"WHO centre at Jamnagar",desc:"The WHO Global Traditional Medicine Centre is established in Gujarat."}],AY_EVIDENCE=[{icon:"✅",title:"Where evidence is reasonably strong",tone:"good",desc:"Yoga and meditation have a substantial trial literature for flexibility, balance, and some measures of stress, anxiety and blood pressure. Several individual plant drugs have well-characterised pharmacology. Dietary and regimen advice overlaps considerably with mainstream lifestyle medicine."},{icon:"❓",title:"Where evidence is thin or mixed",tone:"warn",desc:"Most classical compound formulations have not been tested in adequately powered, well-controlled trials. Many published studies are small, unblinded, or conducted without a comparator, which limits what can be concluded from them either way."},{icon:"⛔",title:"Where the evidence is against",tone:"bad",desc:"Systematic reviews of homoeopathy have not found effects distinguishable from placebo. The dilution premise is inconsistent with established pharmacology. This is stated plainly because describing a system honestly includes describing how its central claim has fared under testing."},{icon:"☠️",title:"Documented safety concerns",tone:"bad",desc:"Some traditional bhasma, kushta and parpam preparations contain mercury, lead or arsenic by design. Products from unverified sources have been linked to heavy-metal poisoning in published case series in several countries. Licensed manufacture under Schedule T exists precisely because this risk is real."},{icon:"⚠️",title:"Interaction risk",tone:"warn",desc:"Herbal preparations can interact with conventional drugs — affecting anticoagulants, antidiabetics and immunosuppressants among others. Anyone taking prescription medication should tell both practitioners what else they are taking."},{icon:"🚫",title:"Substitution risk",tone:"bad",desc:"The most serious documented harm from traditional medicine is not usually the treatment itself but delayed or abandoned treatment for a condition where conventional care is effective and time-sensitive. No system on this page should be used to replace care for a serious or acute illness."}],AY_GALLERY=[{icon:"🌿",title:"Materia Medica",caption:"Plant drugs make up the bulk of Ayurvedic and Unani pharmacy."},{icon:"📜",title:"The Compendia",caption:"Charaka on medicine, Sushruta on surgery, Vagbhata synthesising both."},{icon:"🫙",title:"Dosage Forms",caption:"Churna, kwatha, ghrita, asava — each with its own preparation rules."},{icon:"🧘",title:"Eight Limbs",caption:"Asana is one of eight, and not the first."},{icon:"⚗️",title:"Rasa Shastra",caption:"Mineral and metallic pharmacy — the area where processing standards matter most."},{icon:"🔍",title:"Neikuri",caption:"Oil dropped on urine, read for its spread — a Siddha method with no parallel elsewhere."},{icon:"📕",title:"The Canon",caption:"Ibn Sina's work anchored Unani in India for seven centuries."},{icon:"🏔️",title:"Amchi Practice",caption:"Sowa-Rigpa practitioners across Ladakh, Himachal, Sikkim and Arunachal."},{icon:"💧",title:"Potentisation",caption:"Serial dilution and succussion — homoeopathy's defining and most contested step."},{icon:"🌏",title:"Jamnagar",caption:"The WHO Global Traditional Medicine Centre, established 2022."}],AY_FACTS=["AYUSH stands for Ayurveda, Yoga & Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homoeopathy. Sowa-Rigpa was recognised by statute in 2010, after the acronym was fixed — so the name now under-counts the systems it covers.","Homoeopathy is neither Indian nor ancient. It was founded in Germany around 1796 and reached India in the 1830s, where it was adopted so widely that India now has more homoeopathic practitioners than any other country.","Unani is Greek humoral medicine that outlived its parent. Europe abandoned the four-humour framework; in India the system continued to develop and still trains graduates today.","The Sushruta Samhita describes a forehead-flap rhinoplasty. An account of the technique published in the Gentleman's Magazine in 1794 influenced the development of reconstructive surgery in Europe.","Siddha literature is in Tamil rather than Sanskrit and is attributed to the eighteen Siddhars — which is one reason it is better treated as a distinct system than as a southern branch of Ayurveda.","The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library was built so that patent examiners could find documented Indian traditional knowledge as prior art. It has been used to challenge patents on turmeric and neem.","The WHO Global Traditional Medicine Centre at Jamnagar, established in 2022, is the organisation's only global centre dedicated to traditional medicine.","Yoga was inscribed on the UNESCO Representative List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2016.","In Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, asana is the third of eight limbs and receives only a few verses. The large postural repertoire practised today is mostly a twentieth-century development.","Sowa-Rigpa practitioners are called amchi, and the tradition is practised across Ladakh, Himachal Pradesh, Sikkim, Arunachal Pradesh and the Tibetan exile community.","AYUSH drugs are licensed under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940, with their own good-manufacturing-practice requirements under Schedule T.","Neikuri — dropping sesame oil onto a urine sample and reading how it spreads — is a Siddha diagnostic method with no counterpart in the other five systems."],AY_QUIZ=[{q:"What does the acronym AYUSH stand for?",options:["Ayurveda, Yoga, Unani, Siddha, Homoeopathy","Ayurveda, Yoga, Unani, Sowa-Rigpa, Homoeopathy","Ancient Yogic and Universal Systems of Health","Ayurveda, Yunani, Surgery, Herbalism"],answer:0,explain:"Ayurveda, Yoga & Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homoeopathy. Sowa-Rigpa was recognised in 2010, after the acronym had been fixed, so it has no letter."},{q:"Which AYUSH system did not originate in India?",options:["Siddha","Homoeopathy","Ayurveda","Yoga"],answer:1,explain:"Homoeopathy was founded in Germany by Samuel Hahnemann around 1796 and reached India in the 1830s. Unani is also not Indian in origin — it is Greco-Arabic — so this is a question with more than one defensible answer if Unani were offered."},{q:"Which classical text is the principal surgical compendium of Ayurveda?",options:["Charaka Samhita","Sushruta Samhita","Ashtanga Hridaya","Madhava Nidana"],answer:1,explain:"The Sushruta Samhita. It describes instruments, procedures and reconstructive surgery, including the forehead-flap rhinoplasty."},{q:"What is the theoretical basis of Unani medicine?",options:["The three doshas","The four humours and four qualities","The law of similars","The five elements alone"],answer:1,explain:"Unani works from the Greco-Arabic humoral framework — dam, balgham, safra and sauda — inherited from Hippocratic and Galenic medicine."},{q:"Which system is distinguished by its heavy use of mineral and metallic preparations such as parpam and chendooram?",options:["Siddha","Homoeopathy","Naturopathy","Sowa-Rigpa"],answer:0,explain:"Siddha. Its pharmacy leans further toward processed minerals and metals than any of the other systems."},{q:"When was Sowa-Rigpa given statutory recognition in India?",options:["1970","1995","2010","2014"],answer:2,explain:"2010, through an amendment to the Indian Medicine Central Council Act — four years before the Ministry of AYUSH itself was created."},{q:"In Patanjali's eight limbs, what position does asana occupy?",options:["First","Third","Sixth","Eighth"],answer:1,explain:"Asana is the third limb, after yama and niyama. The Sutras give it only a few verses; the extensive postural practice familiar today developed much later."},{q:"What is neikuri?",options:["A Siddha pressure-point therapy","A Siddha diagnostic method reading oil spread on a urine sample","A Unani cupping technique","An Ayurvedic calcined mineral preparation"],answer:1,explain:"Neikuri is specific to Siddha: sesame oil is dropped onto urine and its spreading pattern is read diagnostically."},{q:"Which of the six systems has no pharmacopoeia at all?",options:["Homoeopathy","Yoga & Naturopathy","Sowa-Rigpa","Unani"],answer:1,explain:"Yoga & Naturopathy. Its interventions are posture, breath, diet, fasting and hydrotherapy — there are no drugs."},{q:"Why was the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library created?",options:["To digitise manuscripts for scholarly research","To give patent examiners searchable prior art and prevent misappropriation of traditional knowledge","To register AYUSH practitioners nationally","To standardise AYUSH drug manufacturing"],answer:1,explain:"The TKDL exists so that patent offices can find documented Indian traditional knowledge as prior art. It was used in challenges to patents on turmeric and neem."},{q:"Which is the most serious documented harm associated with traditional medicine use?",options:["Allergic reactions to plant drugs","Delay or abandonment of effective conventional treatment for a serious illness","Cost of long treatment courses","Difficulty finding qualified practitioners"],answer:1,explain:"Substitution is the principal documented risk. Heavy-metal exposure from unverified mineral preparations is the other well-documented one."},{q:"Where is the WHO Global Traditional Medicine Centre located?",options:["New Delhi","Jamnagar, Gujarat","Geneva","Chennai"],answer:1,explain:"Jamnagar in Gujarat, established in 2022. It is the WHO's only global centre dedicated to traditional medicine."}];